   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to annual reporting on\ninmate substance abuse",
    "basePrintNo": "S6288",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6288-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 21,
       "fullName": "Kevin S. Parker",
       "imgName": "416_kevin_s._parker.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 416,
       "sessionMemberId": 1194,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "PARKER"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §19.07, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6288\n\nSPONSOR: SEPULVEDA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\nannual reporting on inmate substance abuse\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would require the annual reporting of data on the substances\nthat incarcerated individuals are most commonly addicted to and what\ntreatments are made available.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the subdivision (h) of section 19.07 of the mental\nhygiene law.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent New York State law requires the Office of Alcoholism and\nSubstance Abuse Services to monitor substance use disorder treatment\nprograms in state correctional facilities and file an annual report on\nthe adequacy of such programs.\n\nIn New York State correctional facilities, at least 80% of incarcerated\nindividuals have some type of substance use disorder, but data is not\navailable on what the types of substances incarcerated individuals\nabuse. While we work to expand access to treatment within our correc-\ntional facilities, we must have a clear understanding of the most\ncommonly abused substances so we can make available the most appropriate\ntreatment options.\n\nThis legislation would require the annual report on substance use disor-\nder treatment programs in state correctional facilities issued by the\nOffice of Alcoholism and Substance Abuse Services to include data on\nwhat substances incarcerated individuals are most commonly using, what\ntreatments are available and how many individuals are receiving such\ntreatment.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
