    "lawCode": "Amd §19.07, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6288A\n\nSPONSOR: SEPULVEDA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to annual reporting\non substance use disorder in incarcerated individuals\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill would require the Office of Addiction Services and Supports to\nupdate its guidelines for correctional substance use disorder treatment,\nin line with best practices, and would require annual reporting of data\non the substances that incarcerated individuals are most commonly\naddicted to and what treatment they receive.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends the subdivision (h) of section 19.07 of the mental\nhygiene law.\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrent New York State law requires the Office of Alcoholism and\nSubstance Abuse Services to monitor substance use disorder treatment\nprograms in state correctional facilities and file an annual report on\nthe adequacy of such programs.\n\nIn New York State correctional facilities, at least 80% of incarcerated\nindividuals have some type of substance use disorder, but data is not\navailable on what the types of substances incarcerated individuals are\naddicted to. While we work to expand access to treatment within our\ncorrectional facilities, we must have a clear understanding of the most\ncommonly misused substances so we can make available the most appropri-\nate treatment options.  This legislation would require the Office of\nAddiction Services and Supports to update the guidelines it is required\nto provide to the Department of Corrections and Community Supervision,\nbased on best practices for addressing substance use disorder in an\nindividualized manner.\n\nThe legislation would also require the annual report on substance use\ndisorder treatment programs in state correctional facilities issued by\nthe Office of Addiction Services and Supports to include data on what\nsubstances incarcerated individuals are most commonly using, what treat-\nments are available, how many individuals are receiving such treatment,\nat what stage in their incarceration they begin receiving treatment and\nhow many individuals are discharged from treatment, including the\nreasons for the discharge and whether the discharge is connected to drug\nuse and/or placement in solitary confinement.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
